<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">El trovador (Rafael María de Mendive)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Spanish translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Third verse</span><span class="sg-chain-h">added by an unknown author during the American Civil War</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lalla Rookh (William Brough)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1857</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody of the words in a burlesque</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mother Hubbard March (John Philip Sousa)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1885</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of the tune used</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Irish Rhapsody No. 4 (Charles Villiers Stanford)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first verse quoted as preface</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Owen Wingrave (Benjamin Britten)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune quoted and distorted in an opera</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ulysses (James Joyce)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in a novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Finnegans Wake (James Joyce)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Minstrel Boy (Joe Strummer, arr. Hans Zimmer)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in the film Black Hawk Down</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Moreen</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Irish air Moore set it to</span></li></ul></section>
